category: reactors

myth myths-facts-21
title: If something is radioactive, it is automatically dangerous
difficulty: hard
popularity: 7
tags: mythbuster
tags: nuclear
tags: radiation
tags: reactors
tags: hard

sources:
    {"title":"Sources and Effects of Ionizing Radiation","url":"https://www.unscear.org/unscear/en/publications.html","author":"UNSCEAR","type":"official"}
    {"title":"Radiation in Everyday Life","url":"https://www.iaea.org/topics/radiation-in-everyday-life","author":"IAEA","type":"official"}
    {"title":"Safety of Nuclear Power Reactors","url":"https://www.world-nuclear.org/information-library/safety-and-security/safety-of-plants/safety-of-nuclear-power-reactors.aspx","author":"World Nuclear Association","type":"official"}

statement:
    If something is radioactive, it is automatically dangerous.

reality:
    Risk depends on dose, type, and exposure.

explanation:
    This statement is a common myth in nuclear science.

    **Reality:** Risk depends on dose, type, and exposure.

